; A strip holds columns, each a fraction of its length; a column is any
; part, here a column split of two views.
> (set-parts! (layout-scroll:container '() (atom va '(size . 1/2)) (layout-split:container '((direction . column) (size . 1/3)) (atom vb) (atom vc)) (atom vd) (atom ve '(size . 2/3))))
> (layout:select! vb)
> (tree)
(layout:level () (layout-scroll:container () #(views:view ((view . 3) (buffer . "a") (size . 1/2))) (layout-split:container ((direction . column) (size . 1/3)) #(views:view ((view . 4) (buffer . "b"))) #(views:view ((view . 5) (buffer . "c")))) #(views:view ((view . 6) (buffer . "d"))) #(views:view ((view . 7) (buffer . "d") (size . 2/3)))))
; Each column is an item named by the view its focused children lead to.
; Before a client has measured the strip it draws the focused column and
; enough on each side to fill the strip, and is placed to show the
; focused column wholly.
> (strip desktop)
(4 nearest 4 (3 4 6 7))
; The column holding the selected view is the strip's focus; s-l moves
; the focus right, the strip places the new focus for every client, and
; the ends do not wrap.
> (press! desktop '("l" "super"))
> (list (selected) (strip desktop) (strip phone))
(6 (6 nearest 6 (3 4 6 7)) (6 nearest 6 (3 4 6 7)))
